The deeper card message

The change you’re making—from constant recall to event-driven memory injection—is actually consistent with the Living Memory idea.

But you have discovered a second requirement:

Event-driven memory is not the same thing as persistent operating state.

Your system needs both.

Memory retrieval answers:

“What should I remember?”

Protocol reactivation answers:

“How should I operate right now?”

And skill.md answers:

“What is the complete canonical system?”

Those three should not be collapsed into one mechanism.

So my direct recommendation:

Keep skill.md.

Do not reread the whole skill.md at the end.

Add a protocol.md / operational protocol layer and dynamically construct a compact Active Operating State.

Then place that near the end of the context immediately before the model’s response-generation point, especially when your reactivation trigger fires.

That gives you:

canonical knowledge → protocol → active state → end-of-context reactivation → response.

That is the strongest pattern I get from Judgement + Hierophant + Temperance + Emperor.
